{
  "abstract": "Let $H(x)$ be the first shortcut clock on the orbit of $x$ shared with the complete orbit of a smaller positive start, and let $D(x)$ be the first clock below $x$. Put $F(t)=\\rho^t(t+1)^{-3/2}$ with $\\rho=2^{H_2(\\log_3 2)-1}$. In $Y<x<3Y/2$, each of two families has size comparable to $YF(t)$ uniformly through $t\\le\\lfloor\\log_2Y\\rfloor-A$. Both meet after $t$ and descend by $t+J$. One meets at descent with exactly three smaller source-clock pairs. The other meets one step before descent with exactly one pair. For fixed $t$, the natural density of finite coincidences after $t$ is comparable to $F(t)$. For nonnegative integer clocks with $t+s\\le\\lfloor\\log_2Y\\rfloor-A$, the count with $t<H\\le t+J$, $s+1\\le D-H\\le s+8$, and meeting state in $(x,3x/2)$ is comparable to $YF(t)F(s)$. The lower bound holds with a unique smaller pair at visitor clock one. Among starts surviving through $m$, the probability of a low first meeting in a fixed central fraction of the first $m$ steps and descent within the next $J+8$ is comparable to $m^{-1/2}$. The proof combines ternary gain bands, an integer last-exit count, and conditional source equidistribution.",
